---
import DocsPage from "../../layouts/DocsPage.astro"
import { Hands } from "@vectordevai/schema/hands"
import { handsReleasePreview } from "../../data/vector-releases"

export const prerender = true
---

<DocsPage id="vector-hands">
  <p class="note">
    {handsReleasePreview.since} {Hands.NAME} lets Vector work in the desktop apps you approve while you are at your computer.
    Availability depends on your operating system, local engine and permissions.
  </p>
  <p>
    Add <strong>@{Hands.MENTION}</strong> or an app from the composer's Computer group, then describe what you want
    done. Vector can read the approved screen, follow controls and use the pointer and keyboard. It asks before
    entering another app or taking a sensitive step. Text on a page or in an app is information, never your approval.
  </p>

  <h2 id="setup">Set it up</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Open Settings and choose <strong>{Hands.NAME}</strong>, or choose <strong>@{Hands.MENTION}</strong> in a conversation.</li>
    <li>Read the explanation and turn it on. A company policy may keep it off or restrict the available choices.</li>
    <li>On macOS, use the setup buttons to grant Screen Recording and Accessibility to the separately listed helper, then return to Vector.</li>
    <li>Choose the apps for the task. Allowing observation does not automatically allow interaction.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Keep the computer unlocked and remain available for questions. Close private material you do not want a model
    to see. Vector limits captures to approved apps and masks protected windows, but approved app content can still
    include personal information.
  </p>

  <h2 id="permissions">Permissions and setup problems</h2>
  <p>
    On macOS, Screen Recording lets the helper read pixels, and Accessibility lets it inspect and operate controls.
    Post-event access must also be available before keyboard or pointer input. The helper appears separately from
    Vector in System Settings; beta and dev have their own entries. Use the setup buttons to open the relevant pane:
    <a href="x-apple.systempreferences:com.apple.preference.security?Privacy_ScreenCapture">Screen Recording</a>
    or <a href="x-apple.systempreferences:com.apple.preference.security?Privacy_Accessibility">Accessibility</a>.
    Grant access yourself; Vector does not answer an OS permission prompt for you.
  </p>
  <p>
    On Windows, run Vector normally. An administrator window, consent prompt, secure desktop or unavailable capture
    needs a manual handoff. Running Vector as administrator keeps this feature unavailable. Use Windows
    <a href="ms-settings:privacy">Privacy &amp; security</a> when capture is blocked; company restrictions may also apply.
    Windows may show a yellow capture border around an approved window while Vector reads it separately from other apps.
  </p>
  <p>
    Ad-hoc macOS builds are labelled <strong>Preview</strong>. A helper update may cause macOS to ask for access again.
    Only use <strong>Reset and ask again</strong> if Vector offers it; the availability of that button depends on
    verification for your release. Changing an app's verified identity can also require a fresh app approval.
  </p>

  <h2 id="asks">App access and sensitive steps</h2>
  <p>
    App access lasts for the approved task unless you choose an available <strong>Always allow</strong> option.
    Browsers, file managers, finance apps and developer apps always ask; they do not offer a remembered approval.
    You can review and revoke remembered choices in Settings. A company allow rule still needs your consent,
    and a company denial takes priority.
  </p>
  <p>
    Vector asks separately before actions such as sending, deleting, buying, submitting, pasting your clipboard,
    running a program or running code. Each approval covers the proposed step and observed target once.
    Changed targets need another check. For code and other developer text changes, use Vector's editing tools;
    approval does not turn desktop typing into a code editor.
  </p>

  <h2 id="never-list">Apps and fields it never operates</h2>
  <p>
    Terminals, command shells and scripting tools, password managers, remote-control viewers, security and permission
    tools, and Vector's own controls are
    protected. Password and other credential fields require you to enter the information. System consent and
    administrator prompts are yours to complete. These restrictions cannot be removed with Always allow or a
    company app rule. Unsafe console, inspector, scripting and shell-launcher routes remain unavailable inside
    otherwise approved apps too.
  </p>

  <h2 id="stop">Stop and take over</h2>
  <p>
    While Vector acts, a glow and the <strong>{Hands.BANNER}</strong> banner identify the work. Press
    <strong>Stop</strong> in the tray to end it. Move the pointer to pause, then use the banner's
    <strong>Stop</strong> or <strong>Take over</strong> controls. Genuine keyboard, click or significant pointer input pauses a foreground run rather than competing
    with you. Press <strong>Esc</strong> while it works in the foreground, or use the configured stop shortcut.
    The defaults are <strong>Command + Shift + Escape</strong> on macOS and
    <strong>Control + Alt + Shift + Escape</strong> on Windows.
  </p>
  <p>
    Returning after an interruption requires fresh checks. A lost engine connection, missing heartbeat or failed
    input detector stops further input. A locked screen or protected desktop is a handoff, not permission for
    unattended work. For work on another machine, use Vector Remote; it does not grant access to this local desktop.
  </p>

  <h2 id="data-controls">Screenshots, retention and sharing</h2>
  <p>
    Screenshots stay in conversations on this computer and are sent to the model you selected when image input is
    available. A screen-reader turn sends the bounded accessibility text instead. Your provider's data handling
    applies to the content you send it. Screenshots can contain private information even when the task itself is harmless.
  </p>
  <p>
    Settings lets you keep screenshots until the task ends, for a selected period or until you delete the conversation,
    subject to your company's maximum. <strong>Delete all screenshots</strong> removes the local stored screenshots;
    removed history entries stay marked as removed. It cannot retract content already sent to a provider or shared.
    Conversation shares leave screenshots out unless you explicitly choose <strong>Include screenshots</strong>.
    Review them before sharing.
  </p>

  <h2 id="administrators">For administrators</h2>
  <p>
    The managed <code>computer</code> policy requires <strong>a compatible Vector build that includes Hands</strong>. Put it only in an
    authenticated company-managed configuration source described in <a href="/docs/local">Vector Local</a>.
    Do not add it to a repository's or user's <code>vector.json</code>; older engines reject unknown top-level keys.
    Personal preferences belong to the desktop Settings page.
  </p>
  <pre><code>{`{
  "computer": {
    "enabled": true,
    "default_app_access": "ask",
    "allow_always": false,
    "background": false,
    "screenshot_retention_days": 7,
    "native_tools": "off"
  }
}`}</code></pre>
  <p>
    Company policy can disable the feature, deny apps, limit retention and turn off remembered app approvals.
    Enabling the feature or allowing an app in policy never supplies the user's consent.
  </p>

  <h2 id="limits">Supported environments and limits</h2>
  <p>
    Requires macOS 14 or later, or Windows 10 version 2004/build 19041 or later, with a compatible local desktop
    engine. Linux, WSL and remote engines cannot operate this local desktop. Windows foreground operation does not
    imply background support. Locked-session operation is not included. Capture, app providers and keyboard layouts
    can impose further limits; when the target or field cannot be verified, Vector asks you to take over.
  </p>
</DocsPage>
